  "image_name": "1b6e0376c7292a83a13981a9aad35e71.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how astrocytes shape neural circuits and behavior in two parts of the nervous system by comparing normal tissue with astrocyte-specific gene loss. In the hypothalamus, an astrocyte sits between feeding-related neurons, including POMC- and AgRP-expressing cells. In the wild-type condition, the inset suggests fuller astrocyte wrapping or synaptic coverage around a neuronal contact. When leptin receptor function is removed from astrocytes, this coverage is reduced, and the nearby neuron is shown as more active. The listed outcome is increased spontaneous firing and stronger fasting- or ghrelin-induced hyperphagia, implying that altered astrocyte control of synapses promotes feeding signals.\n\nIn the spinal cord, sensory inputs descend onto motor circuits surrounded by astrocytes. The wild-type side shows intact motor neurons and astrocyte networks, whereas loss of Sema3a in astrocytes is associated with disruption of this environment and death of α-motor neurons. The result is reduced peak strength. Overall, the image conveys that astrocytes are not passive support cells: they regulate synaptic organization, neuronal excitability, survival of specific neurons, and ultimately whole-animal functions such as eating behavior and muscle strength.",
